WEBVTT

00:08.876 --> 00:11.876
Subtitling made possible by
Acorn Media

02:33.376 --> 02:36.334
HASTINGS: "Husband put head
in gas oven. Home life happy."?

02:36.459 --> 02:37.709
POIROT: No.

02:38.876 --> 02:41.167
HASTINGS: "Belgravia and
Overseas Bank clerk absconds

02:41.292 --> 02:42.542
with fortune"?

02:43.959 --> 02:45.459
How much is this fortune?

02:45.834 --> 02:47.084
£90, 000.

02:49.709 --> 02:50.584
No.

02:50.709 --> 02:52.542
HASTINGS:
That's a king's ransom, Poirot.

02:52.667 --> 02:54.459
When it is used to ransom
a king,

02:55.084 --> 02:57.459
it becomes interesting
to Poirot.

02:58.542 --> 03:01.001
"Missed typist of 21.
Where is Edna Field?"

03:02.209 --> 03:03.126
Oh, no.

03:03.251 --> 03:05.792
HASTINGS: There's good stuff
here, Poirot.

03:06.334 --> 03:09.001
Mysterious suicide, absconding
bank clerk, missing typist.

03:09.126 --> 03:11.834
Yes, but I am not greatly
attracted to any of them,

03:11.959 --> 03:13.084
mon ami.

03:13.209 --> 03:15.501
I have many affairs
of importance of my own

03:15.626 --> 03:16.501
to attend to.

03:16.626 --> 03:17.834
HASTINGS:
Such as?

03:17.959 --> 03:19.542
My wardrobe, Hastings.

03:21.126 --> 03:24.501
If I mistake not, there is on my
new gray suit a spot of grease.

03:24.626 --> 03:26.542
You have noticed it perhaps,
non?

03:26.751 --> 03:27.667
No.

03:27.792 --> 03:30.584
No? Well, it is only
the one spot, you understand,

03:30.751 --> 03:32.751
but it is sufficient
to trouble me.

03:33.542 --> 03:35.542
Then there's my winter overcoat.

03:36.084 --> 03:38.334
I must lay him aside
in the parlor of kittings.

03:38.459 --> 03:39.709
And I think…

03:40.501 --> 03:42.251
yes, I think the moment is right

03:43.459 --> 03:45.376
for the trimming
of the mustache,

03:46.792 --> 03:48.084
also the pomading.

03:48.251 --> 03:49.417
[ Knock on door ]

03:50.542 --> 03:52.667
There's a lady to see you,
Mr. Poirot.

03:53.376 --> 03:54.626
A lady?

03:54.876 --> 03:57.792
A client.
Her name's Mrs. Todd.

03:59.751 --> 04:02.542
Unless the affair
is one of national importance,

04:02.917 --> 04:04.167
I touch it not.

04:04.459 --> 04:06.417
LEMON: I couldn't say, sir.
Shall I ask her?

04:06.542 --> 04:08.001
No, no, no, no, Miss Lemon.

04:08.584 --> 04:10.917
Show Mrs. Todd in.

04:12.959 --> 04:15.376
We shall judge for ourselves,
eh, Hastings?

04:15.501 --> 04:17.209
HASTINGS:
Oh, yes, yes.

04:19.001 --> 04:20.209
Mrs. Todd.

04:20.334 --> 04:22.626
Doesn't sound as if
she's nationally important.

04:22.751 --> 04:24.167
Mind you,
I knew a Mrs. Jones once

04:24.292 --> 04:26.292
who was Master of Hounds
with the Mid Rutland.

04:26.417 --> 04:27.667
Yes?

04:27.917 --> 04:29.709
No, that's all.
Funny woman.

04:31.501 --> 04:32.751
Mrs. Todd, sir.

04:33.917 --> 04:35.751
Thank you.

04:39.084 --> 04:40.417
Oh, are you Mr. Poirot?

04:42.084 --> 04:44.001
I am Hercule Poirot,
yes, madame.

04:45.292 --> 04:47.417
Oh, you're not a bit
how I thought you'd be.

04:47.542 --> 04:48.626
[ Chuckles ]

04:48.751 --> 04:50.584
Did you pay for that bit
in the paper

04:50.709 --> 04:52.209
saying what a clever detective
you were,

04:52.334 --> 04:54.209
or did they put it in
themselves?

04:54.542 --> 04:55.792
Madame.

04:56.376 --> 04:57.792
Oh, I'm sorry, I'm sure,

04:58.292 --> 05:00.292
but you know what newspapers
are like nowadays.

05:00.417 --> 05:01.626
Nothing but puff.

05:01.751 --> 05:05.209
But no offense taken, I hope.

05:07.834 --> 05:09.667
I'll tell you what I want you
to do for me.

05:09.792 --> 05:11.209
I want you to find my cook.

05:11.334 --> 05:13.584
I fear you are making a mistake,
madame.

05:15.459 --> 05:17.709
Hercule Poirot
is a private detective.

05:18.667 --> 05:19.709
I know that.

05:19.834 --> 05:21.584
Haven't I just told you I want
you to find my cook for me?

05:21.709 --> 05:23.292
Walked out of the house
on Wednesday,

05:23.417 --> 05:25.209
without so much
as a "by your leave, "

05:25.334 --> 05:26.626
and never came back.

05:26.792 --> 05:28.001
I am sorry, madame,

05:28.126 --> 05:31.042
but I do not touch that
particular kind of business.

05:31.626 --> 05:32.876
I wish you good day.

05:34.459 --> 05:35.709
So, that's it, is it?

05:36.584 --> 05:37.834
Too proud, eh?

05:38.292 --> 05:41.251
Only deal with government
secrets and countesses' jewels?

05:41.376 --> 05:44.167
Well, let me tell you,
Mr. High and Mighty Poirot,

05:45.292 --> 05:47.959
a good cook is a good cook,
and when you lose one,

05:49.751 --> 05:53.751
it's as much to you
as pearls are to some fine lady.

05:58.126 --> 06:00.917
Madame, you are in the right,
and I am in the wrong.

06:03.584 --> 06:05.834
Your remarks are just
and intelligent.

06:07.751 --> 06:09.959
This case will be a novelty,
Hastings.

06:11.042 --> 06:13.917
Never before have we hunted
a missing domestique.

06:14.334 --> 06:17.251
Truly, here is the problem
of national importance.

06:18.792 --> 06:20.542
Where do you reside, Mrs. Todd?

06:22.209 --> 06:25.626
88 Prince Albert Road, Clapham.

06:35.209 --> 06:37.334
POIROT: You say this jewel
of a cook went out on Wednesday

06:37.459 --> 06:38.584
and did not return?

06:38.709 --> 06:40.542
Wednesday, yes.
It was her day off.

06:40.667 --> 06:41.917
The day before yesterday.

06:42.042 --> 06:43.501
Has it occurred to you, madame,

06:43.626 --> 06:45.834
that she might have met
with some accident?

06:45.959 --> 06:47.834
Have you inquired
at the hospitals?

06:47.959 --> 06:50.126
That's exactly what I thought
yesterday,

06:50.251 --> 06:53.042
but this morning, if you please,
she sent for her box.

06:53.167 --> 06:54.542
– What box?
– Her box.

06:54.667 --> 06:56.167
HASTINGS:
Her trunk.

06:56.667 --> 06:57.917
Luggage.

06:58.167 --> 06:59.417
Oh, I see.

06:59.959 --> 07:01.917
Will you describe her to me,
madame?

07:02.042 --> 07:03.834
Oh, she's most respectable.

07:04.042 --> 07:05.584
Ten years in her last place.

07:05.876 --> 07:07.709
Middle-aged, gray-haired, thin.

07:07.834 --> 07:09.084
And her name?

07:09.251 --> 07:10.542
Dunn.
Eliza Dunn.

07:11.292 --> 07:13.084
HASTINGS:
This is Clapham.

07:13.251 --> 07:15.542
There's the common.

07:19.292 --> 07:21.126
Charming.

07:24.084 --> 07:26.292
You had no disagreement with her
on Wednesday, madame?

07:26.417 --> 07:28.542
No, that's what makes it all
so queer.

07:29.126 --> 07:30.792
How many servants do you keep?

07:31.042 --> 07:32.042
Two.

07:32.167 --> 07:35.459
Miss Dunn and the house
parlormaid, Annie.

07:38.751 --> 07:40.001
There we are.

07:40.209 --> 07:41.417
There's 88.

07:41.542 --> 07:44.126
All right, driver!

08:05.042 --> 08:06.709
Annie!

08:08.584 --> 08:11.042
Hastings, my friend,
promise me one thing.

08:11.334 --> 08:12.334
What's that, Poirot?

08:12.459 --> 08:15.209
Never, but never
must Chief Inspector Japp hear

08:16.084 --> 08:17.876
that I investigated such a case.

08:18.042 --> 08:19.542
Mum's the word, old boy.

08:25.084 --> 08:27.751
TODD: Now, Annie,
this gentleman's a detective.

08:27.876 --> 08:31.417
He wants to ask you
a few questions.

08:34.292 --> 08:37.292
Here we are, sir.

08:38.834 --> 08:41.542
Voyons, Mademoiselle Annie.

08:43.126 --> 08:44.376
Sit yourself.

08:45.084 --> 08:46.251
Thank you, sir.

08:46.376 --> 08:47.792
All that you shall tell us

08:48.001 --> 08:50.459
will be of the greatest
importance, Annie.

08:50.626 --> 08:52.876
You alone can shed any light
on the case.

08:53.876 --> 08:55.542
Without you, I can do nothing.

08:56.126 --> 08:59.001
Oh, sir, I'm sure I'll tell you
anything I can, sir.

08:59.584 --> 09:00.834
That is good.

09:01.626 --> 09:02.876
Now.

09:05.084 --> 09:06.334
First of all…

09:08.876 --> 09:10.167
what is your own idea?

09:11.042 --> 09:13.501
You are a girl
of remarkable intelligence.

09:13.751 --> 09:16.876
That can be seen at once,
but this is a queer thing, hein?

09:18.167 --> 09:21.376
What is your own explanation
of Eliza's disappearance?

09:22.667 --> 09:24.001
White slavers, sir.

09:24.626 --> 09:25.876
Ah.

09:26.667 --> 09:29.584
I've said so all along, sir,
only no one will listen.

09:30.376 --> 09:32.709
Cook was always warning me
against them.

09:32.959 --> 09:35.959
"Don't you go sniffing no scent
nor eating no sweets,

09:36.626 --> 09:38.876
no matter how gentlemanly
the fellow. "

09:39.626 --> 09:41.959
This is something we have not
thought of, Hastings.

09:42.084 --> 09:43.334
No, right.

09:44.459 --> 09:45.709
Good heavens.

09:47.001 --> 09:49.126
But would she have sent
for her trunk?

09:49.876 --> 09:51.042
Beg pardon, sir?

09:51.167 --> 09:53.417
Miss Dunn sent for her trunk,
I believe.

09:54.334 --> 09:57.334
Now, if she had truly been taken
by these white slavers,

09:57.459 --> 09:59.542
would she have sent
for her luggage?

10:00.251 --> 10:01.667
Well, I don't know, sir.

10:02.209 --> 10:04.334
She'd want her things,
wouldn't she?

10:05.167 --> 10:08.751
Even in foreign parts,
she'd want her things.

10:09.917 --> 10:11.584
Who came for her trunk, Annie?

10:12.167 --> 10:15.042
The carriers, sir,
the men from Carter Patterson.

10:15.709 --> 10:16.959
Did you pack it for her?

10:17.167 --> 10:19.584
No, sir. It was already packed
and corded.

10:19.709 --> 10:21.292
Ah, that is interesting.

10:23.792 --> 10:26.792
That shows that when she left
the house on Wednesday,

10:27.501 --> 10:29.709
already she determined
not to return.

10:31.376 --> 10:32.709
You see that, do you not?

10:33.792 --> 10:35.459
Oh, I'd never thought of that.

10:35.792 --> 10:37.584
No.

10:39.042 --> 10:40.334
Tell me, now, Annie,

10:41.834 --> 10:44.376
what was the very last thing
Eliza said to you

10:46.542 --> 10:47.834
before she went out?

10:48.667 --> 10:51.542
She said, "If there's any
stewed peaches left over

10:53.751 --> 10:56.084
from the dining room,
we'll have them for supper,

10:56.209 --> 10:58.417
and a bit of bacon
and fried potatoes. "

11:00.126 --> 11:02.042
Mad over stewed peaches,
she was.

11:03.626 --> 11:06.501
I shouldn't wonder if that
wasn't the way they got her.

11:06.626 --> 11:07.876
Got her?

11:08.251 --> 11:09.542
The white slavers.

11:10.834 --> 11:12.751
Ah.
The stewed peaches, quite.

11:15.626 --> 11:17.709
POIROT:
Thank you very much, Annie.

11:18.376 --> 11:20.417
You have been most helpful.

11:35.917 --> 11:37.667
Was she saying things about me?

11:37.959 --> 11:39.251
POIROT:
Not at all.

11:39.584 --> 11:41.834
Only things
of the pleasantest nature.

11:42.084 --> 11:44.667
But for a woman of your
intelligence, madame,

11:44.959 --> 11:47.376
it would be tiresome
to bear with patience

11:48.709 --> 11:52.126
the roundabout methods we
plodding detectives have to use.

11:52.292 --> 11:53.542
Oh.
Oh, I see.

11:55.459 --> 11:57.876
Well, I wouldn't say there was
all that much difference

11:58.001 --> 11:59.001
between us, Mr. Poirot.

11:59.126 --> 12:02.042
Mind you, I was always very good
at general knowledge at school.

12:02.167 --> 12:03.042
There.

12:03.167 --> 12:05.751
And your husband, too, I'm sure
is the intellectual, yes?

12:05.876 --> 12:08.209
Yes, yes, he does very well
with figures.

12:09.584 --> 12:11.251
A successful businessman?

12:11.376 --> 12:13.584
With the Prudential, yes,
in the city.

12:14.792 --> 12:17.667
What about the other inmates
of the house, madame?

12:17.917 --> 12:20.292
Oh, you mean Mr. Simpson,
our paying guest.

12:20.417 --> 12:22.292
What is his profession, madame?

12:22.417 --> 12:23.459
Oh, he's in business, too,

12:23.584 --> 12:25.667
with the Belgravia
and Overseas Bank.

12:27.084 --> 12:28.334
Young man, is he?

12:29.001 --> 12:31.001
28, I believe.
A nice young fellow.

12:32.959 --> 12:35.167
Well, they'll both be in
at about 6: 00,

12:36.042 --> 12:37.292
if you want to see them.

12:38.001 --> 12:40.167
An excellent suggestion, madame.

12:41.334 --> 12:43.584
Well, my colleague and I
will take a turn

12:46.001 --> 12:49.542
on your famous common
for one hour.

12:58.251 --> 13:01.501
It's a curious coincidence
that the paying guest Simpson

13:01.834 --> 13:04.501
works in the same bank
as the absconding clerk.

13:05.501 --> 13:07.292
They must have known each other.

13:07.876 --> 13:09.126
Perhaps.

13:10.251 --> 13:12.501
Or possibly
Davis visited Simpson,

13:12.709 --> 13:14.167
fell in love with the cook,

13:14.376 --> 13:17.042
and he persuaded her
to accompany him on his flight.

13:17.167 --> 13:18.751
[ Chuckles ]

13:20.459 --> 13:25.917
Come, Hastings, let us go back
to the prudential Monsieur Todd.

13:32.834 --> 13:36.542
I've heard of you, Mr. Poirot,
haven't I?

13:37.667 --> 13:39.626
It's possible, Monsieur Todd.

13:40.126 --> 13:40.959
Oh, yes.

13:46.501 --> 13:48.001
I follow crime, you know.

13:48.376 --> 13:49.334
Indeed?

13:49.459 --> 13:51.042
Well, perhaps you have
some theories

13:51.167 --> 13:53.292
about the disappearance
of your cook.

13:53.417 --> 13:54.667
Theories?

13:55.001 --> 13:55.876
No.

13:56.001 --> 13:57.459
It's hardly a crime, is it?

13:57.751 --> 13:59.501
A good cook, her, and economic.

13:59.751 --> 14:01.167
I'm very hot in economy.

14:01.376 --> 14:02.792
Admirable.

14:03.501 --> 14:06.751
Now we would like to have a few
words with your Mr. Simpson,

14:08.709 --> 14:11.876
if that's possible.

14:14.167 --> 14:15.667
Another one after that.

14:16.209 --> 14:17.459
Thank you.

14:17.667 --> 14:19.876
Just off the next landing.

14:37.042 --> 14:38.292
[ Knocks ]

14:38.959 --> 14:40.209
Mr. Simpson?

14:40.459 --> 14:42.792
SIMPSON:
Hold on.

14:47.834 --> 14:48.792
Yes?

14:48.917 --> 14:53.251
Mr. Simpson, my name is Poirot.

14:54.959 --> 14:57.792
Hercule Poirot.

14:58.459 --> 14:59.709
Oh.

15:00.501 --> 15:01.751
Yes.

15:04.001 --> 15:06.542
May we have a moment
of your so valuable time?

15:08.126 --> 15:09.584
Certainly.
Come in.

15:11.667 --> 15:13.667
Thank you.

15:20.334 --> 15:21.584
Mr. Simpson,

15:22.042 --> 15:25.042
Mrs. Todd has engaged me
to find Eliza Dunn, the cook.

15:28.459 --> 15:29.709
Oh.
Where is she?

15:31.292 --> 15:33.751
Well, that is what
we do not know, monsieur.

15:35.334 --> 15:36.751
You know her, of course.

15:38.251 --> 15:40.334
Well, I must have met her,
I suppose.

15:44.001 --> 15:48.292
It would seem that Miss Dunn has
not been seen since Wednesday.

15:49.834 --> 15:51.292
Did you see her on that day?

15:51.959 --> 15:53.209
I don't think so.

15:55.501 --> 15:56.751
I don't know.

15:57.501 --> 15:59.751
You were at work as usual,
on Wednesday?

16:01.084 --> 16:02.917
Yes.

16:05.334 --> 16:06.834
Thank you, Mr. Simpson.

16:07.834 --> 16:11.792
It was most kind of you
to spare us the time.

16:15.959 --> 16:17.334
Tell me, Mr. Simpson,

16:19.376 --> 16:21.959
what does a young fellow find
to do around here

16:22.667 --> 16:23.917
of an evening, huh?

16:24.126 --> 16:25.834
Oh, the usual things, you know.

16:25.959 --> 16:28.292
Musical evenings,
amateur theatricals,

16:28.417 --> 16:29.667
that kind of thing?

16:30.376 --> 16:31.626
Yes, I suppose so.

16:32.376 --> 16:34.376
You do not interest yourself,
hmm?

16:36.251 --> 16:37.501
Afraid not.

16:37.709 --> 16:39.876
No.

16:41.292 --> 16:43.667
Thank you.

16:56.084 --> 16:58.959
HASTINGS: I think that husband
has got something to do with it.

16:59.084 --> 17:00.501
To do with the missing cook?

17:00.626 --> 17:03.084
There's something about him
I don't trust.

17:03.709 --> 17:06.584
Merely because a man does not
offer you a drink, Hastings,

17:06.709 --> 17:08.792
does not mean
that he is necessarily

17:09.459 --> 17:11.209
guilty of other crimes.

17:13.417 --> 17:15.959
But there doesn't seem to be
any crime at all,

17:16.126 --> 17:17.251
as far as I can see.

17:17.376 --> 17:23.709
No, it is a curious case,
full of contradictory features.

17:24.376 --> 17:25.626
I am interested.

17:26.334 --> 17:32.417
Oh, yes.
I am distinctly interested.

17:34.376 --> 17:36.459
What?!

17:36.584 --> 17:37.459
What?!

17:37.584 --> 17:38.834
How does she dare?

17:39.001 --> 17:40.251
What is it?

17:40.626 --> 17:42.167
As a favor, as a great favor,

17:43.167 --> 17:46.209
I agreed to investigate this
two-penny, half-penny affair.

17:46.334 --> 17:47.209
What is it, old chap?

17:47.334 --> 17:49.209
Read it! Read it!

17:50.501 --> 17:52.209
"Mr. Todd regrets that,
after all,

17:52.334 --> 17:55.459
his wife will not avail herself
of Mr. Poirot's services.

17:55.584 --> 17:57.459
After talking the matter over
with me,

17:57.584 --> 18:00.001
she sees that it is foolish
to call in a detective

18:00.126 --> 18:01.542
about a purely domestic affair.

18:01.667 --> 18:04.126
Mr. Todd encloses a guinea
for consultation. "

18:04.251 --> 18:05.542
Is this to be believed?

18:05.667 --> 18:08.917
Do they think they can get rid
of Hercule Poirot like that?

18:09.501 --> 18:10.376
No.

18:10.501 --> 18:11.751
No, no, no, no, no, no.

18:13.501 --> 18:14.751
36 times no.

18:16.001 --> 18:17.584
They send me one guinea, huh?

18:17.917 --> 18:18.584
No!

18:18.709 --> 18:20.251
I will spend my own guineas.

18:20.876 --> 18:22.292
3, 600 of them, if need be.

18:23.751 --> 18:27.042
But I will get to the bottom
of this matter.

18:29.376 --> 18:30.626
If Eliza Dunn…

18:32.209 --> 18:38.209
will communicate
with this address…

18:40.042 --> 18:42.459
… she will hear something
to her profit.

18:44.917 --> 18:45.792
Advantage.

18:45.917 --> 18:47.126
– Yes?
– Yes.

18:47.251 --> 18:48.376
Good.

18:48.501 --> 18:50.501
– You have that, Miss Lemon?
– Yes, Mr. Poirot.

18:50.626 --> 18:52.709
Put it in all the papers
you can think of.

18:52.834 --> 18:54.959
She won't read The Times,
I shouldn't think.

18:55.084 --> 18:57.459
No, my dear Miss Lemon,
perhaps she will not,

18:57.584 --> 18:59.667
but perhaps her new employers
will.

19:00.084 --> 19:02.751
Now run along, Miss Lemon.
Vite, vite.

19:03.917 --> 19:07.167
And you, Hastings, do not you
run away with such celerity.

19:07.292 --> 19:08.584
I have work for you, too.

19:08.709 --> 19:09.709
Oh, as a matter of fact,

19:09.834 --> 19:12.209
I was thinking of popping down
to Sandown this afternoon.

19:12.334 --> 19:13.792
No, no, no.
You do not pop.

19:14.167 --> 19:17.001
There's a horse running
a pal of mine earns a leg of.

19:17.126 --> 19:19.251
When he owns four legs,
I pop with you.

19:20.376 --> 19:21.959
But now is time for work, yes?

19:23.792 --> 19:25.126
I want you to telephone

19:25.417 --> 19:27.626
all the domestic agencies
you can find

19:28.292 --> 19:34.042
and see if they have found
Miss Eliza Dunn a new post.

19:35.126 --> 19:41.042
As for me…I pop to the city
of London.

19:45.459 --> 19:48.251
And Mr. Simpson was at work
as usual on Wednesday?

19:49.042 --> 19:51.834
Oh, yes, Mr. Simpson
is an excellent timekeeper.

19:53.084 --> 19:57.417
To tell the truth, he was absent
on Thursday with a cold.

19:58.792 --> 20:01.459
Well, you have been
most helpful, Mr. Cameron.

20:01.667 --> 20:02.709
Thank you.

20:02.834 --> 20:05.167
Glad to be of assistance.

20:10.459 --> 20:12.167
It is an unfortunate occurrence,

20:12.292 --> 20:14.417
this business
of Davis absconding.

20:14.542 --> 20:16.459
Oh, you heard about that,
did you?

20:16.792 --> 20:18.709
Well, one could scarcely
miss it.

20:18.959 --> 20:21.376
The newspapers love scandals
about that.

20:22.542 --> 20:24.751
Ah, that is human nature,
Mr. Cameron.

20:25.126 --> 20:27.376
But it is comforting
for us mere mortals

20:28.084 --> 20:30.959
to know that banks, too,
have their difficulties.

20:31.542 --> 20:34.167
Again, I thank you, monsieur.

20:45.501 --> 20:47.376
JAPP:
Chief Inspector Japp.

20:47.584 --> 20:50.001
I wonder if I might ask you
a few questions.

20:51.584 --> 20:54.459
You and Davis are friends,
I believe, Mr. Simpson.

20:55.042 --> 20:56.292
That's right.

20:56.834 --> 20:58.376
I can't believe this of him.

21:00.042 --> 21:01.709
There must be some mistake.

21:02.084 --> 21:05.876
If I told you, sir, the number
of times I've heard that said.

21:09.084 --> 21:11.751
If you had to hazard
a conjecture, Mr. Simpson,

21:12.042 --> 21:14.584
as to where Davis might go
if he wanted to hide,

21:14.709 --> 21:16.042
where would you say?

21:18.376 --> 21:19.626
I -- I don't know.

21:20.459 --> 21:23.667
He was fond of Broadstairs.

21:24.209 --> 21:26.001
Yes, I was thinking of somewhere

21:26.209 --> 21:28.292
a little more exotic than that,
sir.

21:28.626 --> 21:30.084
Foreign parts, even.

21:30.917 --> 21:33.167
Oh, I don't think
he's ever been abroad.

21:34.584 --> 21:36.792
You were at work as usual
on Wednesday,

21:37.876 --> 21:39.292
were you, Mr. Simpson?

21:39.501 --> 21:42.084
It's Thursday
you want to know about, surely,

21:42.542 --> 21:44.001
and I was away on Thursday.

21:44.334 --> 21:45.459
No, no, Mr. Simpson.

21:45.584 --> 21:48.626
Although the bonds were found
to be missing on Thursday,

21:48.751 --> 21:50.792
we believe that they must have
been removed from the bank

21:50.917 --> 21:52.001
on Wednesday.

21:52.126 --> 21:55.209
I was here all day Wednesday.

21:57.167 --> 21:58.417
Now, sir…

21:59.251 --> 22:01.251
That man.

22:05.209 --> 22:06.626
JAPP:
What about him?

22:07.417 --> 22:09.167
He was at my dig-ins yesterday.

22:09.292 --> 22:12.584
Was he, by George?

22:32.834 --> 22:33.917
Ah.

22:34.042 --> 22:35.834
My dear Chief Inspector Japp.

22:36.626 --> 22:38.417
After the reward, eh, Poirot?

22:38.542 --> 22:40.084
Hmm?
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

22:40.876 --> 22:43.251
No, I am engaged
on quite a different case.

22:43.376 --> 22:45.667
And what would
this different case be, then?

22:45.792 --> 22:49.376
Ah, well, now, there is such a
thing as client confidentiality,

22:50.709 --> 22:52.001
Chief Inspector.

22:53.001 --> 22:55.459
Let me just say
that this case I am engaged on

22:56.042 --> 22:57.792
is of national importance.

22:58.834 --> 23:00.709
Well, I'm glad to hear it,
Poirot.

23:00.834 --> 23:02.292
Someone was trying to tell me

23:02.417 --> 23:04.792
you'd gone into
the missing domestics business.

23:04.917 --> 23:06.084
No, no, I said.

23:06.209 --> 23:07.459
Not Poirot, I said.

23:07.709 --> 23:10.251
Hard times or not,
he wouldn't fall that far.

23:11.251 --> 23:13.001
[ Chuckles ]

23:36.751 --> 23:38.834
No word from Eliza Dunn,
Miss Lemon?

23:39.042 --> 23:42.251
No, Mr. Poirot.

24:01.084 --> 24:03.251
Another letter
from the Duchess of Braxton.

24:03.376 --> 24:04.626
No, no, no.

24:05.126 --> 24:06.626
She sounds desperate.

24:07.792 --> 24:09.667
No.

24:18.959 --> 24:21.251
Good heavens.

24:28.959 --> 24:29.917
Mr. Poirot.
Mr. Poirot.

24:30.042 --> 24:31.417
Yes, Miss Lemon, yes?

24:31.834 --> 24:33.959
There's a letter, sir.
A letter from Eliza Dunn.

24:34.084 --> 24:35.334
There!

24:36.251 --> 24:37.667
Did I not tell you, Hastings?

24:37.792 --> 24:40.667
And you tried to waste Poirot's
time with the duchesses.

24:40.792 --> 24:41.792
Well, I only thought…

24:41.917 --> 24:43.959
You must learn, my friend,
of patience.

24:44.084 --> 24:45.084
Read it to us, Miss Lemon.

24:45.209 --> 24:46.376
"Dear Sir or Madam,

24:46.501 --> 24:49.167
ref. your add in the newspaper,
I already got my legacy,

24:49.292 --> 24:50.792
if there's some mistake.

24:50.917 --> 24:52.334
Yours truly, Eliza Dunn, Miss.

24:52.459 --> 24:54.792
P.S. Thanks all the same
for your trouble."

24:54.917 --> 24:56.709
What does she mean, "legacy"?

24:57.251 --> 24:59.126
We must talk to this woman,
at…

25:02.251 --> 25:04.292
Fell Cottage, Amdale, Kiswick.

25:04.459 --> 25:05.334
Kiswick?

25:05.459 --> 25:08.167
That's Keswick, Mr. Poirot,
in the Lake District.

25:08.292 --> 25:10.292
Ah.
Are there trains to this place?

25:10.417 --> 25:11.501
Oh, yes.

25:11.626 --> 25:12.501
Bon.

25:12.626 --> 25:14.959
Come, Hastings.
We have a train to catch.

25:15.459 --> 25:16.709
But --

25:25.667 --> 25:27.542
POIROT:
Look at it, Hastings.

25:28.042 --> 25:29.459
Not a building in sight.

25:31.209 --> 25:34.126
Not a restaurant, not a theater,
not an art gallery.

25:38.834 --> 25:40.084
A wasteland.

25:42.042 --> 25:43.834
I thought you liked the country.

25:44.126 --> 25:46.334
But this is not the country,
my friend.

25:46.792 --> 25:50.126
The country is full of trees
and flowers and public houses.

25:52.667 --> 25:54.876
This is a desert.

25:56.001 --> 25:58.501
HASTINGS: I don't know why
we've come all this way, anyway.

25:58.626 --> 25:59.584
You've found the cook.

25:59.709 --> 26:02.167
POIROT: The cook is but
the beginning of the story,

26:02.292 --> 26:03.501
Hastings.

26:03.626 --> 26:06.834
We're on to bigger things.

26:14.167 --> 26:16.334
[ Train whistle blows ]

26:20.042 --> 26:22.584
[ Sheep bleating ]

26:23.792 --> 26:26.042
Look at that, Poirot.
Look at that view!

26:28.292 --> 26:30.709
Yes, well,
views are very nice, Hastings,

26:31.876 --> 26:33.792
but they should be painted
for us,

26:35.167 --> 26:36.459
so that we may study them

26:36.584 --> 26:38.917
in the warmth and comfort
of our own homes.

26:39.042 --> 26:40.542
That is why we pay the artist,

26:40.667 --> 26:43.459
for exposing himself to these
conditions on our behalf.

26:43.584 --> 26:46.292
What do you mean, "conditions"?
It's a wonderful day.

26:46.417 --> 26:47.292
[ Inhales deeply ]

26:47.417 --> 26:49.417
Just fill your lungs
with that air.

26:49.876 --> 26:51.126
No, my poor friend.

26:51.417 --> 26:54.959
This sort of air is intended for
birds and little furry things.

26:55.959 --> 26:57.251
The lungs of Hercule Poirot

26:57.376 --> 26:59.209
demand something
more substantial.

26:59.334 --> 27:02.376
The good air of the town.

27:05.584 --> 27:09.126
Fell Cottage, I perceive.

27:14.209 --> 27:16.209
We have got the right
Eliza Dunn, have we?

27:16.334 --> 27:18.501
I mean, what is she doing
all the way up here?

27:18.626 --> 27:21.834
That is what we are here
to find out.

27:26.917 --> 27:28.751
HASTINGS:
Wonderful position.

27:28.876 --> 27:32.751
If you are a rock,
it's wonderful.

27:33.792 --> 27:34.667
Yes?

27:34.792 --> 27:36.042
Miss Eliza Dunn?

27:36.834 --> 27:37.751
Yes.

27:37.876 --> 27:40.459
Formerly in the employ
of Mrs. Ernestine Todd

27:41.209 --> 27:43.209
at 88 Prince Albert Road,
Clapham?

27:43.751 --> 27:44.792
That's right.

27:44.917 --> 27:46.667
I am Hercule Poirot, Miss Dunn.

27:47.709 --> 27:51.084
You were kind enough to answer
my newspaper advertisement.

27:51.209 --> 27:52.459
Oh, yes.

27:53.334 --> 27:55.292
Well, like I said in my letter,
sir,

27:55.417 --> 27:57.084
I've already got my legacy.

27:57.584 --> 28:00.209
Miss Dunn, may we be permitted
to come into the house

28:00.334 --> 28:01.126
for a moment?

28:01.251 --> 28:03.334
I'm sure your employer
will not object.

28:03.459 --> 28:05.251
[ Laughs ]
No, she won't mind.

28:06.417 --> 28:07.292
Come in.

28:07.417 --> 28:08.334
Thank you.

28:08.459 --> 28:09.959
Come in.

28:16.459 --> 28:18.584
The reason I laughed, sir,
I'm sorry,

28:19.667 --> 28:21.542
is I don't have no employer,
see.

28:22.584 --> 28:23.834
This is my house.

28:25.167 --> 28:26.709
Oh, Miss Dunn, forgive me.

28:27.709 --> 28:30.126
That's all right, sir.
You wasn't to know.

28:30.334 --> 28:32.001
This is part of my legacy, see.

28:33.126 --> 28:34.376
Oh, sit down. Do.

28:36.001 --> 28:37.751
Thank you.

28:44.042 --> 28:46.792
Now, Miss Dunn,
you have mentioned your legacy,

28:49.167 --> 28:52.084
but Captain Hastings and I,
we know nothing of this.

28:53.876 --> 28:55.042
What was the ad, then?

28:55.167 --> 28:58.376
Your late mistress, Mrs. Todd,
was much concerned about you.

28:58.501 --> 29:00.584
She feared some accident
might have befallen you.

29:00.709 --> 29:02.292
But didn't she get my letter?

29:02.751 --> 29:04.042
No, she got no letter.

29:05.751 --> 29:07.209
She got no word of any kind.

29:07.542 --> 29:08.792
Oh, dear, sir.

29:09.751 --> 29:11.667
I gave the letter
to Mr. Crotchet.

29:12.292 --> 29:15.209
I said most particular
he was to give it to Mrs. Todd.

29:15.917 --> 29:17.167
Oh, dear.

29:18.334 --> 29:21.334
Perhaps you would recount
to Captain Hastings and me

29:21.834 --> 29:23.084
the whole story.

29:24.042 --> 29:25.792
Well, Wednesdays is my day out.

29:30.001 --> 29:32.876
And it was last Wednesday
that it all started like.

29:33.042 --> 29:35.376
MAN: The stone
which the builders rejected,

29:35.501 --> 29:37.917
the same has become
the head of the corner.

29:38.626 --> 29:41.084
DUNN: I'd been over to see
my friend in Oxton

29:41.334 --> 29:44.251
and I was on my way home,
when a gentleman stopped me.

29:45.542 --> 29:48.001
He said he'd been asking for me
at number 88.

29:49.417 --> 29:50.751
And he told me some story

29:51.292 --> 29:54.292
about an old friend of
my grandmother's in Australia

29:55.667 --> 29:58.209
leaving me a house
and some money in her will.

29:59.667 --> 30:01.751
Well, I didn't believe him
at first.

30:02.042 --> 30:03.959
And then he showed me
this letter.

30:04.626 --> 30:08.126
It will be the stone which
the builders have rejected…

30:10.334 --> 30:12.876
Who was these lawyers,
Hearst and Crotchet?

30:14.167 --> 30:16.126
127 Wentworth Way, Melbourne?

30:19.251 --> 30:24.626
Well, one of them
is right here, Miss Dunn.

30:26.167 --> 30:27.751
"Benjamin Crotchet.

30:29.084 --> 30:30.792
Attorney-at-law."

30:31.751 --> 30:34.667
I hope that allays
your very understandable fears.

30:36.042 --> 30:37.417
Oh, yes, indeed, sir.

30:39.251 --> 30:40.709
I didn't mean to doubt you.

30:41.834 --> 30:43.542
Now, Miss Dunn, to business.

30:44.084 --> 30:46.626
I do have to tell you
that the house is in Keswick.

30:46.751 --> 30:48.834
That's just over by Acton,
isn't it, sir?

30:48.959 --> 30:52.292
No, Miss Dunn. It's in the north
of England, near Carlisle.

30:52.834 --> 30:54.084
Oh, my good God.

30:54.584 --> 30:57.334
But before we confront
that particular difficulty,

30:57.459 --> 30:58.584
there is one other stipulation

30:58.709 --> 30:59.917
that we should get
out of the way.

31:00.042 --> 31:01.376
What ever is that, sir?

31:01.542 --> 31:03.251
Well, it's of no importance,

31:03.501 --> 31:06.167
as it doesn't apply
in this case, thank heaven.

31:06.792 --> 31:08.292
It's just a stipulation

31:08.417 --> 31:10.584
that you should not be
in domestic service.

31:10.709 --> 31:13.251
Oh.

31:14.542 --> 31:16.542
What ever is the matter,
Miss Dunn?

31:16.751 --> 31:18.001
I am a cook, sir.

31:20.042 --> 31:21.959
Didn't they tell you
at the house?

31:22.917 --> 31:24.709
My dear Miss Dunn,
I had no idea.

31:29.042 --> 31:32.042
This is very unfortunate.

31:34.834 --> 31:36.751
Will I have to lose the money,
sir?

31:37.959 --> 31:39.209
And the house, sir?

31:40.376 --> 31:41.709
It sounds ever so nice,

31:42.667 --> 31:44.459
even if it is a bit
out of the way.

31:47.459 --> 31:48.709
I believe I have it.

31:51.001 --> 31:53.126
We lawyers always know
a thing or two.

31:54.917 --> 31:58.001
The way out here is for you
to have left your employment

32:00.501 --> 32:01.751
before we met.

32:02.126 --> 32:04.126
But we have met, sir,
and I haven't.

32:05.042 --> 32:06.376
– Haven't you?
– No.

32:06.834 --> 32:08.334
Haven't you, Miss Dunn?

32:09.709 --> 32:11.167
No.

32:15.001 --> 32:16.501
Oh, I see.

32:17.959 --> 32:19.209
Yes.

32:20.251 --> 32:23.167
You left your employment
this morning, did you not,

32:23.709 --> 32:24.959
before we met.

32:25.917 --> 32:27.209
Yes, I remember now.

32:29.917 --> 32:31.334
I did, as a matter of fact.

32:32.542 --> 32:33.792
There.

32:34.542 --> 32:35.917
Now, it is imperative

32:37.334 --> 32:39.834
that you take possession of the
house by noon tomorrow.

32:39.959 --> 32:41.209
In order to do that,

32:41.834 --> 32:44.084
you must catch the night train
from King's Cross.

32:44.209 --> 32:45.167
Oh!

32:45.292 --> 32:46.626
Come along, Miss Dunn.

32:46.751 --> 32:48.876
I can advance you £10 or so
for the fare

32:49.376 --> 32:52.501
and you can write a note
to your employer at the station,

32:52.667 --> 32:55.126
which I shall
personally deliver for you.

32:59.584 --> 33:02.126
And was everything
as Mr. Crotchet had said?

33:03.959 --> 33:05.292
Oh, yes, and more, sir.

33:06.334 --> 33:08.126
Now, what about your luggage?

33:09.459 --> 33:12.001
All the things you had left
at 88 Prince Albert Road?

33:12.126 --> 33:15.334
Oh, well, Mr. Crotchet sent them
on, like he said he would.

33:15.459 --> 33:17.876
But it was all done up
in brown paper, like.

33:18.251 --> 33:19.501
I don't know.

33:20.334 --> 33:22.459
I suppose Mrs. Todd
was angry with me,

33:23.376 --> 33:24.917
and grudged me my bit of luck

33:25.167 --> 33:27.042
and wouldn't let him have
my box.

33:27.334 --> 33:30.209
– But Annie said the trunk was --
– Later, my friend.

33:30.542 --> 33:31.792
Later.

33:32.292 --> 33:35.876
DUNN: But there you said
she never got my letter.

33:37.042 --> 33:39.751
Well, I can't say
as I blame her.

33:42.084 --> 33:43.334
I see.

33:45.876 --> 33:47.626
Thank you, mademoiselle.

33:48.042 --> 33:49.167
Oh.

33:49.292 --> 33:50.751
There has been, as you say,

33:52.251 --> 33:54.334
a little "muddle"
about your trunk,

33:55.334 --> 33:57.292
which I will straighten out
with Mrs. Todd,

33:57.417 --> 33:58.292
if you will permit.

33:58.417 --> 34:00.001
Oh, thank you very much, sir.

34:01.001 --> 34:02.251
Come, Hastings.

34:03.709 --> 34:09.584
We must return to London
with all possible speed.

34:13.667 --> 34:16.792
Is there nowhere you can contact
Chief Inspector Japp?

34:17.542 --> 34:19.292
Then telephone him at his home.

34:20.251 --> 34:22.042
Yes, of the greatest urgency.

34:22.751 --> 34:25.209
Tell him he should not
be looking for Davis!

34:25.417 --> 34:27.334
He should be looking
for Simpson!

34:28.167 --> 34:31.834
No, not Davis, but Simpson!

34:52.459 --> 34:54.792
– Thank you.
– Thank you.

35:00.417 --> 35:02.959
Why should Japp want Simpson
all of a sudden?

35:03.917 --> 35:06.042
What's Simpson
supposed to have done?

35:10.084 --> 35:11.542
Hastings, my friend,

35:12.501 --> 35:15.376
the little gray cells
are not working today, hein?

35:16.042 --> 35:18.334
They take a little vacances,
hein?

35:19.751 --> 35:21.834
It's to do with this Eliza,
isn't it?

35:22.459 --> 35:23.709
POIROT:
No.

35:24.251 --> 35:25.501
Oh.

35:25.626 --> 35:27.751
We have moved
beyond the petty sphere

35:28.084 --> 35:30.209
of cooks from Clapham, Hastings.

35:31.542 --> 35:34.042
Then, it's something to do
with this Australian fellow?

35:34.167 --> 35:36.042
There is no Australian fellow.

35:37.001 --> 35:39.501
Yes, there is.
She told us about him.

35:42.667 --> 35:45.251
Do you remember
when we interviewed Simpson?

35:45.542 --> 35:47.167
Interviewed Simpson?

35:48.417 --> 35:49.459
No.

35:49.584 --> 35:52.042
In his little room,
at the house in Clapham.

35:53.251 --> 35:54.959
Oh, right.
The lodger, yes.

35:55.334 --> 35:56.792
Do you remember I asked him

35:57.251 --> 35:59.917
if he took an interest
in amateur theatricals?

36:00.292 --> 36:02.542
HASTINGS:
Yes, I do, as a matter of fact.

36:02.709 --> 36:05.084
Why do you think
I asked him that?

36:06.167 --> 36:07.417
Why?

36:08.501 --> 36:12.084
Well, making conversation,
I suppose.

36:14.584 --> 36:15.626
I asked him that

36:15.751 --> 36:18.667
because he had recently
been wearing a false beard.

36:19.084 --> 36:21.417
He had a tiny trace
of gum Arabic in his…

36:23.209 --> 36:24.542
What do you call it here?

36:24.792 --> 36:25.792
Sideburn.

36:25.917 --> 36:28.584
Sideburn, yes.

36:30.459 --> 36:32.292
Do you see?

36:34.376 --> 36:35.626
Oh, right.
Yes.

36:37.376 --> 36:40.209
Bon.

36:45.251 --> 36:48.584
The Australian was Simpson
in a false beard.

37:18.126 --> 37:19.376
Hey!

37:19.959 --> 37:22.376
[ Doorbell rings ]

37:24.792 --> 37:25.959
Who are you?

37:26.084 --> 37:27.376
I am Hercule Poirot.

37:28.792 --> 37:30.042
Who are you?

37:30.667 --> 37:33.209
Sarge, there's some
French gent at the door.

37:34.501 --> 37:37.042
No, no, no, no, no,
I am not some "French gent. "

37:37.167 --> 37:38.501
I am some Belgian gent.

37:39.167 --> 37:41.501
Well, well, well,
if it isn't Mr. Poirot.

37:41.792 --> 37:43.001
It is.

37:43.126 --> 37:44.584
Is the lady of the house in?

37:45.292 --> 37:46.709
The chief inspector is here.

37:46.834 --> 37:48.084
TODD:
Mr. Poirot.

37:48.209 --> 37:49.792
Ah, Mrs. Todd.
Good morning.

37:49.917 --> 37:51.834
I don't know how you have
the impertinence

37:51.959 --> 37:53.292
to show your face here again!

37:53.417 --> 37:56.584
You were paid off, Mr. Poirot.
You were paid off handsomely.

37:56.709 --> 37:58.334
– Well…
– And then look at this.

37:58.459 --> 38:00.334
We've got policemen crawling
all over the house!

38:00.459 --> 38:03.251
JAPP: You're not too popular
in Clapham this morning, Poirot.

38:03.376 --> 38:05.084
A bit of a wild goose chase,
this.

38:05.209 --> 38:08.126
As far as we can ascertain,
this Mr. Simpson of yours

38:08.917 --> 38:11.667
is a perfectly respectable
young bank employee

38:11.876 --> 38:14.334
who happens to have gone home
to his family.

38:14.459 --> 38:17.042
We have the Shropshire
Constabulary checking that

38:17.167 --> 38:18.042
at the moment.

38:18.167 --> 38:19.459
I only wanted to ask --

38:19.834 --> 38:20.667
No!

38:21.792 --> 38:23.334
Well, perhaps you'd be
interested to know --

38:23.459 --> 38:24.709
No, I wouldn't.

38:24.917 --> 38:26.792
Good day, Mr. Poirot.

38:29.626 --> 38:31.917
Poirot.

38:58.626 --> 39:00.626
Annie, I need to ask you
a question.

39:01.667 --> 39:02.917
Can you help me?

39:03.042 --> 39:04.209
I'll try, sir.

39:04.334 --> 39:06.376
Last Friday,
the Carter Patterson

39:06.501 --> 39:08.584
came to collect Eliza's trunk,
yes?

39:09.292 --> 39:10.542
Her box.
Yes, sir.

39:11.251 --> 39:13.459
– You were here when he came?
– Oh, yes, sir.

39:13.584 --> 39:15.251
I had to show them up
to Eliza's room

39:15.376 --> 39:16.459
for them to take it up.

39:16.584 --> 39:17.542
Bon.

39:17.667 --> 39:19.042
It was ever so heavy, sir.

39:19.167 --> 39:22.834
It took three of them
to get it down the stairs.

39:24.334 --> 39:25.584
You all right, sir?

39:26.626 --> 39:27.876
Of course.

39:29.501 --> 39:32.417
Tell me, Annie, you said
her box was already packed.

39:33.917 --> 39:36.334
Oh, yes, sir.
Packed, locked, and corded.

39:37.251 --> 39:40.167
Quite a thick rope round it
and done up ever so tight.

39:41.042 --> 39:42.376
Did it have a label on it?

39:42.834 --> 39:44.084
Yes, it did, sir.

39:44.626 --> 39:45.876
With an address?

39:47.167 --> 39:48.417
Just her name.

39:48.834 --> 39:50.334
Miss Eliza Dunn, it said.

39:51.459 --> 39:53.667
Twickenham Station,
to be called for.

39:55.542 --> 39:56.792
Very well, Annie.

39:57.209 --> 39:58.292
Thank you.

39:59.417 --> 40:00.667
Au revoir.

40:00.834 --> 40:02.667
Au revoir, sir.

40:03.584 --> 40:07.501
Ah, excellent!

40:16.626 --> 40:19.084
Then why would Crotchet
want to perpetrate

40:19.417 --> 40:20.751
such an elaborate hoax?

40:20.876 --> 40:23.917
Ah. That is a very good
question, Hastings.

40:26.167 --> 40:28.042
But Eliza Dunn
has got her house.

40:28.542 --> 40:29.834
I shall be surprised

40:30.417 --> 40:33.209
if she finds she has more
than a six months' lease.

40:34.001 --> 40:36.292
– [ Bell rings ]
– So, what did Crotchet want?

40:36.417 --> 40:37.292
Crotchet?

40:37.417 --> 40:39.542
Well, Simpson, then,
pretending to be Crotchet.

40:39.667 --> 40:42.001
He wanted something
that Eliza Dunn had.

40:43.209 --> 40:45.126
Money, the Australian legacy?

40:45.251 --> 40:47.251
He wanted a battered
old tin trunk.

40:48.126 --> 40:50.459
HASTINGS:
Her trunk, with nothing in it?

40:51.001 --> 40:52.251
[ Bell rings ]

40:52.376 --> 40:53.667
But he could buy a trunk.

40:53.792 --> 40:56.167
Oh, but he did not want
a new trunk, mon ami.

40:56.292 --> 40:57.959
He wanted a trunk of pedigree,

40:58.459 --> 41:00.709
a trunk of assured
respectability.

41:01.626 --> 41:04.834
Look here, Poirot, what would
Simpson want an old trunk for?

41:04.959 --> 41:08.376
To put the body in, of course.

41:09.959 --> 41:12.917
[ Train whistle blows ]

41:16.084 --> 41:18.834
Sent on to Glasgow, gov,
to await collection there.

41:18.959 --> 41:20.251
But how do you know this?

41:20.376 --> 41:22.417
You have not consulted
your records.

41:22.542 --> 41:25.084
I don't need to consult
my records, gov, do I?

41:25.417 --> 41:27.334
What do you mean, body?
What body?

41:27.626 --> 41:29.292
– Is he with you?
– Whose body?

41:30.001 --> 41:30.876
I mean to say,

41:31.001 --> 41:32.292
if there's going to be bodies
all over the place…

41:32.417 --> 41:35.209
Doucement, Hastings.
All will be revealed to you.

41:35.334 --> 41:37.376
Can we get on with the business
in hand?

41:37.501 --> 41:38.751
By all means.

41:39.292 --> 41:40.834
I saw to it myself, didn't I?

41:41.459 --> 41:42.917
Yesterday morning.

41:43.084 --> 41:44.417
Some cove with a beard.

41:45.334 --> 41:46.209
Crotchet.

41:46.334 --> 41:47.417
You spoke to this man?

41:47.542 --> 41:48.709
Of course I spoke to him.

41:48.834 --> 41:50.084
Did he give a name?

41:50.209 --> 41:52.751
Nah, he just said
this Eliza Dunn was his aunt

41:53.626 --> 41:56.084
and she wanted the trunk sent on
to Glasgow.

41:56.376 --> 41:58.167
And it's gone?
You have sent it?

41:58.667 --> 41:59.584
No.

41:59.709 --> 42:00.584
Every Friday,

42:00.709 --> 42:02.917
the Southern Railway pays me
huge amounts of money

42:03.042 --> 42:04.834
so I won't do
that sort of thing.

42:05.042 --> 42:07.251
I think he's being sarcastic,
Poirot.

42:07.542 --> 42:08.792
No, no, Hastings.

42:10.209 --> 42:11.459
He is a good man.

42:12.292 --> 42:15.959
He is doing the job
of great responsibility.

42:16.917 --> 42:19.501
Thank you, monsieur.

42:20.542 --> 42:22.584
I'll tell you one thing, though.

42:28.709 --> 42:30.376
And what is that, monsieur?

42:32.042 --> 42:33.917
You looking for this bloke
or something?

42:34.042 --> 42:35.709
Well, of course
we're looking for him.

42:35.834 --> 42:37.876
I'm talking to the engineer,
not the oil rag.

42:38.001 --> 42:39.251
Now, look here!

42:39.501 --> 42:40.751
Hastings.

42:41.126 --> 42:43.917
What is this one thing
you will tell us, monsieur?

42:45.376 --> 42:46.626
All right.

42:48.209 --> 42:49.459
I'll tell ya.

42:51.001 --> 42:52.417
I'll tell you where he is.

42:53.959 --> 42:55.584
There.

42:57.001 --> 42:58.251
Go on.

42:58.751 --> 43:00.209
Well, at least rough figures.

43:00.334 --> 43:01.584
Ah, a bit less sure now.

43:01.751 --> 43:03.626
I bet he is.
I bet he's where I say.

43:03.792 --> 43:06.209
And where do you say?

43:07.584 --> 43:08.834
Bolivia. There.

43:09.917 --> 43:11.167
Bolivia?

43:11.417 --> 43:13.001
That's in South America.

43:13.792 --> 43:16.667
And what is it that makes
you think he is in Bolivia?

43:17.126 --> 43:19.667
Well, going there,
at least on his way, like.

43:21.876 --> 43:25.001
He has to pay for this trunk
to be sent on to Glasgow, see?

43:25.876 --> 43:26.959
I see.

43:27.084 --> 43:28.542
He takes out a wad of notes.

43:28.876 --> 43:30.876
Could choke a cart horse,
it would.

43:31.209 --> 43:33.126
There's a few English notes
there,

43:33.251 --> 43:35.167
which he pays
what he owes me with,

43:35.751 --> 43:37.417
but I couldn't help but notice

43:38.042 --> 43:41.376
most of the other notes
were Bolivian.

43:43.959 --> 43:46.167
Hastings,
this gentleman is a genius.

43:48.334 --> 43:50.334
How do you know
they were Bolivian?

43:50.751 --> 43:52.501
Well, one tiny little thing was

43:53.834 --> 43:58.292
they had "Bolivia" written
all over them, didn't they?

44:03.792 --> 44:06.459
Here we are --
this week's sailings.

44:09.167 --> 44:10.417
Ah.

44:10.542 --> 44:12.292
Nothing for Bolivia today.

44:12.626 --> 44:15.167
Bolivia is a landlocked country,
Hastings.

44:15.292 --> 44:16.751
Ah, that explains it, then.

44:16.876 --> 44:19.834
So one sails to Buenos Aires
and goes by train from there.

44:19.959 --> 44:21.584
What lovely names they all have.

44:21.709 --> 44:23.959
"Queen of Heaven
sailing for Caracas. "

44:24.709 --> 44:25.667
Thank you, Miss Lemon.

44:25.792 --> 44:27.751
Can we stick to Buenos Aires,
please?

44:27.876 --> 44:30.459
Ah, here we are.

44:32.126 --> 44:34.917
The S. S. Nevonia,
from Southampton, at 1:00 a.m.

44:36.126 --> 44:37.542
Well, let's go, then.

44:40.584 --> 44:44.501
Are we looking for Simpson
or the trunk?

44:45.251 --> 44:46.584
Simpson, of course.

44:46.876 --> 44:48.292
The trunk is in Glasgow.

44:49.417 --> 44:53.709
But first, Scotland Yard.

45:06.584 --> 45:08.584
And of course you see
some sinister point

45:08.709 --> 45:10.709
in all this Arthur Simpson's
activities.

45:10.834 --> 45:13.084
POIROT:
Yes, of course, Chief Inspector.

45:13.209 --> 45:15.792
Six months' rent on a house,
£150 to Miss Dunn.

45:17.126 --> 45:19.792
It is not much to assure
the success of his plan.

45:21.542 --> 45:22.959
And this famous plan is?

45:23.334 --> 45:24.792
I mean, we've heard a lot
about it, Mr. Poirot,

45:24.917 --> 45:26.376
and I'm sure it's been
very entertaining.

45:26.501 --> 45:28.709
We are wasting time,
Chief Inspector.

45:29.334 --> 45:30.709
[ Sighs ]
Go on, then.

45:31.667 --> 45:34.251
His plan, of course,
is to steal £90, 000 worth

45:34.959 --> 45:36.542
of negotiable securities

45:36.667 --> 45:39.459
from the Belgravia
and Overseas Bank.

45:41.292 --> 45:42.167
No.

45:42.292 --> 45:44.126
That's Davis.

46:03.459 --> 46:05.126
MAN:
Here it is, Sergeant.

46:06.292 --> 46:07.709
[ Telephone rings ]

46:08.501 --> 46:10.542
Chief Inspector Japp speaking.

46:10.709 --> 46:12.001
Ah, yes, Sergeant.

46:12.834 --> 46:14.084
Good, good.

46:15.667 --> 46:17.709
It's Glasgow.
They found the trunk.

46:17.834 --> 46:18.792
And?

46:18.917 --> 46:20.334
What do you mean, "and"?

46:20.876 --> 46:22.751
Have they opened it?

46:24.751 --> 46:28.959
Sergeant Hendry,
have you opened the trunk?

46:29.959 --> 46:31.209
Ah, yes, I see.

46:33.209 --> 46:35.459
Apparently they need a warrant
on them.

46:35.626 --> 46:39.167
Mon Dieu.
There is a body in that trunk.

46:40.709 --> 46:43.042
A body.

46:44.126 --> 46:46.501
Look, Sergeant, there seems
to be the possibility

46:46.626 --> 46:48.209
there's a body in that trunk.

46:48.334 --> 46:49.584
Yes, a dead body.

46:50.834 --> 46:52.084
You will?

46:53.251 --> 46:54.542
Right.
Thank you.

46:55.334 --> 46:57.459
He thinks
that may expedite matters.

46:57.751 --> 46:58.959
He's going to ring me back.

46:59.084 --> 47:01.001
Now, then, what body?
Whose body?

47:01.501 --> 47:02.751
Davis, of course.

47:03.751 --> 47:05.417
This is nonsense, Poirot.

47:05.667 --> 47:07.751
Davis stole those securities
from the bank,

47:07.876 --> 47:10.334
Davis is the one
who disappeared the day…

47:10.626 --> 47:11.876
after.

47:12.542 --> 47:14.126
POIROT:
Precisely.

47:14.751 --> 47:17.209
You think what Simpson
wanted you to think.

47:19.251 --> 47:21.001
Listen, Chief Inspector.

47:21.459 --> 47:24.709
On Wednesday, Simpson,
disguised, decoys away the cook.

47:28.459 --> 47:31.667
Now, he has already removed
the securities from the bank,

47:31.792 --> 47:34.209
but knows that this
will not be discovered

47:34.792 --> 47:36.417
until Thursday afternoon.

47:36.542 --> 47:38.959
Now, he does not go to the bank
on Thursday.

47:40.167 --> 47:43.417
Instead he lies in wait for
Davis when he comes out to lunch

47:43.876 --> 47:46.126
and asks him to come to Clapham
with him.

47:47.959 --> 47:49.542
Now, it is the maid's day out.

47:51.459 --> 47:52.876
Mrs. Todd is at the shops.

47:54.001 --> 47:55.542
There is no one in the house.

47:57.584 --> 48:02.126
Simpson kills Davis.

48:11.626 --> 48:13.542
The one difficulty
for a murderer

48:14.542 --> 48:16.084
is the disposal of the body.

48:18.626 --> 48:24.084
And that is why Simpson wanted
the trunk of Miss Eliza Dunn.

48:30.959 --> 48:33.542
And now, if I am not much
mistaken, my friends,

48:35.751 --> 48:39.834
it is here we will apprehend
our murderer.

48:47.209 --> 48:49.626
Have I got something wrong,
Chief Inspector?

48:49.751 --> 48:53.251
No, no, Poirot.
Can't be right every time.

48:58.959 --> 49:00.209
Mon Dieu, I am wrong.

49:03.001 --> 49:05.334
I am wrong.

49:06.501 --> 49:07.751
Officer.

49:08.626 --> 49:10.917
Where does the Queen of Heaven
sail from tonight?

49:11.042 --> 49:12.959
Why, from pier 5, sir,
over there.

49:13.126 --> 49:14.792
Poirot, where are we going?

49:15.667 --> 49:18.334
The Queen of Heaven
sails for Caracas tonight.

49:18.834 --> 49:21.126
I remember it from The Times.

49:24.042 --> 49:26.376
Yes, but Caracas
isn't in Bolivia, is it?

49:27.084 --> 49:30.001
What the porter saw on
the bank note was not Bolivia.

49:30.376 --> 49:32.459
– What was it, then?
– It was bolivar.

49:32.709 --> 49:35.709
And the bolivar is the unit
of currency in Venezuela.

49:36.042 --> 49:38.876
Our friend is on his way
to Venezuela!

49:51.876 --> 49:54.667
Simpson!

49:59.376 --> 50:00.959
Stop that man!

50:14.834 --> 50:16.667
No, no, no, no, no, no,
Miss Lemon.

50:16.792 --> 50:17.751
To the left.

50:17.876 --> 50:19.376
At least one centimeter
to the left.

50:23.584 --> 50:25.001
That's better.

50:31.459 --> 50:32.459
Voilà.

50:32.584 --> 50:34.917
Is there nothing
to which Hercule Poirot

50:35.042 --> 50:36.542
cannot turn his finger?

50:36.667 --> 50:38.584
Hand.

50:41.876 --> 50:44.667
Pay to Hercule Poirot,
the sum of one guinea only.

50:46.334 --> 50:47.584
Ernest Todd.

50:48.792 --> 50:51.042
It is to me, Hastings,
a little reminder

50:52.376 --> 50:54.459
never to despise the trivial,
hein,

50:54.751 --> 50:56.084
or the undignified.

50:57.126 --> 50:59.251
A disappearing domestic
at one hand,

50:59.792 --> 51:03.751
a cold-blooded murder
at the other.

51:08.167 --> 51:11.167
Subtitling made possible by
Acorn Media